// RestoreFromCompanyTrash Bring an item back from the company's Trash
//
// ONE CALL, AND IT IS BACK WHERE IT WAS, with its history. A folder returns with everything
// that went with it. Something that only went with a deleted folder is not brought back
// alone: `404`, and so is anything that is not in the company's Trash.
//
// SOMETHING IN TRASH HOLDS NO NAME, so the name it had may be taken since. Bringing it back
// onto a taken name is refused with `name_taken`, and the call can carry another `name`.
// Sent `{}`, it comes back under the name it had.
//
// WHERE IT WAS MAY BE IN TRASH TOO. The call is then refused with `folder_in_trash`, which
// names that folder in `trashed_folder`, and asks for a `parent_id`: a company folder. Sent
// one, the item returns THERE, and the folder in Trash stays in Trash.
//
// A `parent_id` IS HONOURED WHENEVER IT IS SENT, and is held to what a move in the company's
// tree holds one to: `parent_not_found` for a place that is no company folder you may bring
// it back into, `parent_not_a_folder`, `managed_by_app` for the company's System Storage, and
// `moves_into_itself`. The top of the company's storage is not a place to send yet, as it is
// not for a move; an item that was at the top still comes back there when no place is sent.
//
// ITS OWN PERMISSION, asked about the item: deleting something and bringing it back are two
// acts, and holding the one is not holding the other.
