// RestoreFromTrashWithBody Bring an item back from Trash
//
// ONE CALL, AND IT IS BACK WHERE IT WAS, with its history.
//
// WHOLE OR NOT AT ALL. A folder returns with everything that went with it. Something that
// only went with a deleted folder is not brought back alone: `404`, and so is anything that
// is not in Trash.
//
// SOMETHING IN TRASH HOLDS NO NAME, so the name it had may be taken since. Bringing it back
// onto a taken name is refused with `name_taken`, and the call can carry another `name`.
// Sent `{}`, it comes back under the name it had.
//
// WHERE IT WAS MAY BE IN TRASH TOO: an item deleted by itself, and its folder deleted
// afterwards. It has no place to go back to, so the call is refused with `folder_in_trash`,
// which names that folder in `trashed_folder`, and asks for a `parent_id`: a folder of
// yours, or your tree's id for the top. Sent one, the item returns THERE, and the folder in
// Trash stays in Trash. Nothing is put anywhere you did not choose.
//
// A `parent_id` IS HONOURED WHENEVER IT IS SENT, as another `name` is: the item comes back
// into that folder even when where it was is still there. The place is held to what a move
// holds one to — `parent_not_found` for a folder that is not among yours or is in Trash,
// `parent_not_a_folder`, `managed_by_app` for System Storage, and `moves_into_itself`.
//
// A FOLDER KEEPS ITS RULES IN TRASH, AND A GIT DOOR THERE IS NOT OPEN, so another may have been
// opened above or below it since. Coming back into the line of an open door, it or a door it
// holds is refused (`git_door_inside_git_door`, which names the other door in `open_git_door`):
// it stays in Trash, and nothing closes a door on its own. Close one of them, or send a
// `parent_id`.
//
// AN APP'S OWN OBJECT COMES BACK THROUGH ITS APP. Restore is Storage's button, and the app that
// owns the object answers first. Taken back, it returns whole. When the app cannot take it
// back, the call is refused with `kept_by_its_app`, `kept_by` names the app and says why in its
// own words, and it stays in Trash. When the app cannot be asked, `owning_app_unavailable`, and nothing
// changed. An app that does not answer this yet answers `501 not_implemented` (ONPLO-5724).
//
// ITS OWN PERMISSION, asked about the item: deleting something and bringing it back are two
// acts, and holding the one is not holding the other.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type.
//
// Corresponds with POST /personal-storage/trash/{item_id}/restoration (the `RestoreFromTrash` operationId).
func (c *Client) RestoreFromTrashWithBody(ctx context.Context, itemId ItemId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewRestoreFromTrashRequestWithBody(c.Server, itemId, contentType, body)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

