	// in parts, each sent straight to the store — and every step names the same folder.
	//
	// THE LIMIT IS THE COMPANY'S AND IS KNOWN BEFORE YOU START: a file over it is stopped here
	// with `file_too_large`, before a byte is sent. `allow_over_limit_once` changes nothing in a
	// business account environment: a file passes it once by naming an allowed request in
	// `upload_request_id`, as a member's own upload does.
	//
	// THE SPACE IS THE COMPANY'S: a file in company storage counts against the company's storage,
	// never a member's own, and `quota_full` names it: `full_storage` is `company_storage`.
	//
	// THE TOP OF THE TREE TAKES NO FILE. A top-level company folder asks who gets in when it is
	// made, and a file has nobody to ask: the tree's id names no folder here, and is answered
	// `parent_not_found`. So is any id in a personal account environment, which has no company
	// storage.
	//
	// A NAME ALREADY CARRIED THERE IS REFUSED, AND THE REFUSAL NAMES NOTHING. "Replace" is
	// `startCompanyVersionUpload` on the file that carries the name, which the folder's list
	// shows whoever may read the folder: this call asks only the create of it.
	//
	// THE FILE'S ID EXISTS BEFORE ITS BYTES DO, and an upload nobody finishes is swept after a
	// week, as a person's own is.
	//
	// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
	//
	// Corresponds with POST /company-storage/folders/{parent_id}/uploads (the `StartCompanyUpload` operationId).
