// ListFavoritesWithResponse What you starred
//
// EVERYTHING YOU STARRED across your personal storage — files, folders and app items — in
// one list.
//
// SOMETHING YOU CAN NO LONGER REACH LEAVES THIS LIST rather than sitting in it failing to
// open. A shortcut to something you cannot reach is worse than no shortcut. Its star is
// kept: the item is back in the list the moment you can reach it again.
//
// THE SAME LIST AS ANYWHERE ELSE IN STORAGE, so it is in a folder's order — folders first,
// then by name — and each item answers what its folder would list for it. What an app owns is
// on System Storage and in no list here (decision 1033).
//
// AN ARCHIVED ITEM IS LEFT OUT unless `include_archived` asks for it, as in every list.
// This operation had no such switch until the service was built against the shared rule:
// "default lists HIDE archived items; every list carries a “Show archived” switch".
//
// A PAGE MAY HOLD FEWER ITEMS THAN `limit` AND STILL NOT BE THE LAST: an item that left the
// list is not replaced on its page. `next_cursor` is the only end signal.
//
// EACH ROW IS ASKED ITS READ: the file's for a file, the folder's for a folder — which is
// what makes something you can no longer reach leave this list. The STAR is yours alone;
// what it points at is seen as anything else is.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /personal-storage/favorites (the `ListFavorites` operationId).
