// IssueReadAddressWithResponse Ask for an address to read a file's bytes
//
// WHAT A PREVIEW AND A DOWNLOAD BOTH FETCH. A page loads a file in a second request, and an
// image slot has nowhere to put a credential. So you prove yourself once, here, and are
// handed an address that carries its own proof and then dies.
//
// THE ADDRESS IS A CREDENTIAL WHILE IT LIVES. Never put it in a log, a referrer or an
// analytics trail. The check happened when it was ISSUED, so a permission revoked seconds
// later still resolves until the window closes — which is why the window is minutes and
// never hours, and why the address is refused in any other account environment.
//
// A PAGE HELD OPEN PAST THE WINDOW ASKS AGAIN, and draws the gap as deliberate rather than
// broken.
//
// A SEALED FILE IS AN ORDINARY ANSWER, NOT AN ERROR: `sealed` is true, there is no address,
// and a screen draws a stated refusal.
//
// IT ALWAYS READS THE NEWEST VERSION. A folder has no bytes of its own (`not_a_file`).
//
// A `POST` because every call mints a new address.
//
// YOU NEED THE FILE'S READ. Your own file needs nothing granted; a colleague or a guest
// reads it when it, or a folder above it, was shared with them.
//
// A FILE AN APP PLACED IS READ WITH THAT APP'S OWN READ, never with Storage's: an invoice's
// PDF opens to whoever may see the company's invoices.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /personal-storage/items/{item_id}/read-address (the `IssueReadAddress` operationId).
