// own words, and it stays in Trash. When the app cannot be asked, `owning_app_unavailable`, and nothing
// changed. An app that does not answer this yet answers `501 not_implemented` (ONPLO-5724).
//
// ITS OWN PERMISSION, asked about the item: deleting something and bringing it back are two
// acts, and holding the one is not holding the other.
//
// Takes a body of the `application/json` content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /personal-storage/trash/{item_id}/restoration (the `RestoreFromTrash` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) RestoreFromTrashWithResponse(ctx context.Context, itemId ItemId, body RestoreFromTrashJSONRequestBody, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*RestoreFromTrashResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.RestoreFromTrash(ctx, itemId, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseRestoreFromTrashResponse(rsp)
}

// StartUploadWithBodyWithResponse Start uploading a file
//
// THE BYTES NEVER COME THROUGH THIS API. Starting an upload reserves the file's place and
// answers how it goes up: in parts of `part_bytes` each, every part sent straight to the
// store at an address this API hands out one at a time.
//
// THE LIMIT IS KNOWN BEFORE YOU START, never discovered at ninety-seven per cent. It is
// `max_file_bytes` on the meter and on `getUploadLimit`. A file over it is stopped here,
// before a byte is sent, with `file_too_large`: the problem's `over_limit` carries the file's
// size and the limit, for the screen to say in plain words. It is a limit you set, never
// Onplo's ceiling (decision 0758).
//
// TWO WAYS ON FROM THAT STOP. Change your limit (`setUploadLimit`) and start again, or start
// again with `allow_over_limit_once: true`. Allowing once changes nothing for the next file:
// it is stopped again. Neither lifts `quota_full`: a file must fit in the space left.
//
// IN A BUSINESS ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT THE LIMIT IS THE COMPANY'S, and `allow_over_limit_once`
// changes nothing there. A file passes it once by naming an allowed request in
// `upload_request_id`: one you allowed yourself (`allowUploadOnce`), or an ask of yours a
// colleague allowed (`askToPassUploadLimit`, `answerUploadLimitRequest`). A request that is
// not yours, not allowed, already spent, or for a smaller file lets nothing through, and the
// file is stopped with `file_too_large` as before.
//
// THE STOP SAYS WHETHER ANYBODY MAY ALLOW IT, in a business account environment only:
// `over_limit`'s `anybody_may_allow` is `false` where nobody in the company holds Approve an
// upload over the limit, and the screen says "Nobody here may allow a file over the limit
// yet" and offers no ask. It names nobody. Identity answers it inside your request
// (`IsEnvironmentPermissionHeld`, decision 1019); where identity gives no answer, the field
// is left out, never read as `false`.
//
// THE FILE'S ID EXISTS BEFORE ITS BYTES DO. Until the upload is completed the file answers
// as absent, holds no quota, and appears in no list.
//
// AN UPLOAD NOBODY FINISHES IS SWEPT after a week, parts and all. Nothing has to be
// cancelled.
//
// INSIDE SYSTEM STORAGE THIS IS REFUSED with `managed_by_app`. An upload always makes a new
// file of your own.
//
// ONE NAME PER PLACE. A name already carried where the file would land is refused with
// `name_taken`, before a byte is sent, and so is the finish when the name was taken while the
// file was going up. "Keep both" is a start under another name, such as "Report (2).pdf";
// "Replace" is `startVersionUpload` on the file that carries the name. The problem's `name_taken_by`
// names what carries it — its id, its name as typed, and whether it is a file or a folder —
// so a screen can offer the choice without a second read.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /personal-storage/uploads (the `StartUpload` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) StartUploadWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*StartUploadResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.StartUploadWithBody(ctx, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseStartUploadResponse(rsp)
}

