// GetRetentionWithResponse How long earlier versions and trashed items are kept
//
// TWO WINDOWS, ONE FOR THE WHOLE ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT. The version-history window is how long
// an earlier version is kept automatically: one dial between nothing and 30 days, 30 by
// default (decision 0498). The Trash auto-clean window is how many days a deleted item waits
// in Trash before it is removed: 7 to 30, 30 by default. Named versions are never thinned,
// whatever the window, and nor is a version a deploy, an open branch or an open proposal
// points at.
//
// WHOSE THEY ARE. In a personal account environment that is you. In a business one they are
// set once for the whole environment, because there it is the company's storage and one
// person's preference is not the company's policy.
//
// HOW FAR EACH MAY GO is in the answer, `longest_version_history_days` and
// `longest_trash_days`: 30 for each, and 3650 — ten years — for each with Extended windows
// (history & trash), a Premium feature (decision 0965). The page offers choices up to a year
// and a Custom length up to ten years; either is sent here in days.
//
// YOU NEED `storage:retention-policy.read`, held across the account environment (decision
// 0811): the rules are the environment's, never a folder's.
//
// WHEN ACCOUNT CANNOT BE ASKED who holds Storage's Premium seat, the answer is 503
// `account_unavailable`, never the free windows in their place: not known is not "not held".
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /retention (the `GetRetention` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) GetRetentionWithResponse(ctx context.Context, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*GetRetentionResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.GetRetention(ctx, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseGetRetentionResponse(rsp)
}

// SetRetentionWithBodyWithResponse Change how long earlier versions and trashed items are kept
//
// BOTH WINDOWS, SENT TOGETHER, for every item in the account environment. A longer window
// brings back nothing already removed.
//
// A VERSION-HISTORY WINDOW OF 0 TURNS HISTORY OFF. The page warns first, in plain language:
// "Mistakes can no longer be undone after Trash expires". Trash keeps its own window, so
// history off never means a delete is instant.
//
// LONGER THAN 30 DAYS needs Extended windows (history & trash), a Premium feature. Without it
// the answer is 409 `needs_premium`, and nothing changed. With it, each window goes up to 3650
// days, ten years (decision 0965); longer than that is nobody's, and the answer is 422.
//
// YOU NEED `storage:retention-policy.change`, held across the account environment. Changing
// does not carry seeing (decision 0811), and nothing is answered beyond what you just set
// and how far each window may go.
//
// WHEN ACCOUNT CANNOT BE ASKED who holds Storage's Premium seat, the answer is 503
// `account_unavailable`, never 409 `needs_premium` in its place. Nothing changed, and the
// same request can be sent again. EVERY CHANGE ASKS ACCOUNT, a change within 30 days too,
// because the answer says how far each window may go, and only Account can say that. This
// said until ONPLO-4332 that a change within 30 days asks Account nothing.
//
// NOT HERE: history on or off for one app or one object. The Version history page names it,
// and the catalogue places only the environment's windows under this permission (ONPLO-2556).
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with PUT /retention (the `SetRetention` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) SetRetentionWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*SetRetentionResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.SetRetentionWithBody(ctx, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseSetRetentionResponse(rsp)
}

